<p>Voters in England, Scotland and Wales went to the polls on Thursday in the largest electoral test for British Prime Minister Keir Starmer since Labour won power in 2024. The contests include about 5,000 English local council seats and several mayoral races, all 129 seats in the Scottish Parliament and 96 seats in the Welsh Senedd; polls opened at 7 a.m. and were due to close at 10 p.m. local time. Labour faces projections of heavy losses after falling in national polling and facing criticism over weak economic growth, strained public services, living costs and Starmer&#x27;s judgment, including his appointment of Peter Mandelson as Britain&#x27;s ambassador to Washington. Opposition parties have cast the vote as a referendum on Starmer&#x27;s government, and heavy losses could prompt Labour lawmakers to challenge his leadership before the next general election, which must be held by 2029. Nigel Farage&#x27;s Reform UK is expected to make gains in parts of England and Wales, while the Greens are targeting Labour-held urban areas including London, Plaid Cymru is challenging Labour in Wales and the Scottish National Party appears positioned to remain the dominant party in Scotland. Some councils will count ballots overnight, but most results are expected on Friday afternoon, with final declarations continuing into Saturday.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/UpzQ5ZvbnG6HuG1TV-A1LG3dnIAmUkdgZl0GYJ28LciwowZJESf6bFaThK-VirD5PGJp2WHPIa2cgz8MDpbVtq6mAEzOc2XhXGhlIyjRo064M-2QhdtCVcnxDQjva56Qnfp87C_B6PlkqqMQ5dF8Dx5PNz9yE1dYCpOCsGKPpCzgYT5sK2acnfaf9QcXSK_OYvpeQtYF7UxpdOQti0HMaiJlETzABJj1R2-6kJ8q3CI' alt='Prime Minister Keir Starmer at the launch of Labour&#x27;s local election campaign in Wolverhampton, England, in March.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Labour exposure: Labour is defending about 2,500 English council seats, with forecasts ranging from losses of well over half of them to about 1,850 seats.</li><li>Welsh contest: Polls indicated Labour could lose the national vote in Wales for the first time in more than a century, with Plaid Cymru and Reform UK challenging for first place.</li><li>Conservative losses: The Conservatives, led by Kemi Badenoch, also faced projected losses, including estimates that they could lose about 600 English council seats as Reform UK targets former Conservative areas.</li><li>London polling: An Omnisis poll for Byline Times put Labour on 28% in London, down from 42% at the capital&#x27;s 2022 local elections, and found only half of Labour&#x27;s 2024 general-election voters planned to back the party in English local contests.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Keir Starmer and Labour: Starmer&#x27;s closing appeal named Reform UK&#x27;s Nigel Farage and Green leader Zack Polanski as rivals he said were not fit for a period of global instability. He said Labour would put Britain&#x27;s national interest first. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2026/may/06/starmer-late-pitch-voters-labour-greens-reform-elections'>The Guardian</a>)</li><li>Reform UK: Reform UK campaigned with the slogan &quot;Vote Reform, Get Starmer Out,&quot; and Farage said strong results would mean Starmer was gone by midsummer. (<a href='https://www.independent.com.mt/articles/2026-05-06/world-news/Local-elections-could-hasten-the-exit-of-Britain-s-embattled-prime-minister-6736289447'>The Malta Independent</a>)</li><li>More in Common pollster Luke Tryl: Tryl said Starmer had become a focus for public disappointment and disillusionment less than two years after Labour&#x27;s 2024 landslide. (<a href='https://www.independent.com.mt/articles/2026-05-06/world-news/Local-elections-could-hasten-the-exit-of-Britain-s-embattled-prime-minister-6736289447'>The Malta Independent</a>)</li><li>LSE professor Tony Travers: Travers said Labour faced a four-way fight in England and a five-way fight in Wales and Scotland, with different challengers taking votes in different places. (<a href='https://www.bastillepost.com/ho